        <body>
            <div xml:id="d946">

                <pb facs="0902" n="588" sameAs="frus:frus1914Supp/pg588"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72111G27/20</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The German Ambassador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Bernstorff</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Counselor for
                        the Department of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">German Embassy</hi>, <lb/>
                    <placeName>
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                        calendar="gregorian" when="1914-11-11">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">November 11, 1914</hi>.</date>
                    <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received November 12</hi>.] </dateline>
                <p rendition="#flush">J. No. A 2171]</p>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">My dear Mr. Lansing</hi>: I beg to acknowledge receipt of your letter of
                    7th instant informing me that the German steamer <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Locksun</hi>, belonging to the Norddeutscher Lloyd, now at Honolulu, is
                    considered by the United States Government as being a tender to H. M. S. <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Geier</hi> and would therefore be interned at Honolulu
                    if she did not leave immediately.</p>
                <p>In reply to this communication I beg you to kindly let me know on which rule or
                    regulation the detention of the <hi rendition="#italic">Locksun</hi> is to be
                    based.</p>
                <p>The <hi rendition="#italic">Locksun</hi> can not be considered as a man-of-war,
                    not even an auxiliary ship, but is a simple merchant ship. As to the alleged
                    coaling of H. M. S. <hi rendition="#italic">Geier</hi> from the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Locksun</hi>, the neutrality regulations of the United
                    States only provide that a vessel can be prevented from taking coal to a warship
                    for a period of three months after having left an American port. As the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Locksun</hi> left the last American port (Manila) on
                    August 16 she ought to be free on November 16.</p>
                <p>I am [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">J. Bernstorff</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
